    {
      "type": "intuition",
      "term": "optics-m4-polarizer-light-types",
      "title": "Which light changes as a polarizer turns",
      "meaning": "Rotating a polarizer changes the transmitted intensity differently for each polarization state.",
      "points": [
        "<b>Unpolarized</b> and <b>circular</b>: intensity stays constant.",
        "<b>Plane-polarized</b>: zero and maximum twice per turn.",
        "<b>Partially polarized</b>: \\(I_{\\max}\\) and \\(I_{\\min}\\), two of each per turn.",
        "<b>Elliptical</b>: \\(I_{\\max}\\) and \\(I_{\\min}\\) on the ellipse axes."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "info",
      "label": "05 · §20.13 Light types",
      "title": "Rotating a polarizer: each light type",
      "points": [
        "<b>Unpolarized</b>: half the intensity passes; constant on rotation.",
        "<b>Partially polarized</b>: varies from \\(I_{\\max}\\) to \\(I_{\\min}\\); two of each per turn.",
        "<b>Plane-polarized</b>: zero to maximum twice in one full rotation.",
        "<b>Circular</b>: two equal perpendicular parts; intensity constant on rotation.",
        "<b>Elliptical</b>: \\(I_{\\max}\\) along the semi-major axis, \\(I_{\\min}\\) along the semi-minor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "math",
      "label": "§20.9 Law",
      "title": "Malus' law",
      "tex": "I = I_1\\cos^2\\theta",
      "points": [
        "<b>I</b>: transmitted intensity; <b>\\(I_1\\)</b>: intensity of the light entering the analyzer.",
        "<b>\\(\\theta\\)</b>: angle between the light's plane of polarization and the transmission axis.",
        "Unpolarized input: \\(I_1 = I_0/2\\) (Eq. 20.9), so \\(I = \\frac{I_0}{2}\\cos^2\\theta\\) (Eq. 20.10).",
        "\\(\\theta = 0^\\circ, 180^\\circ\\): \\(I = I_1\\). \\(\\theta = 90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\\): \\(I = 0\\)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "label": "Quiz · OMR",
      "time": 60,
      "q": "Analyzer axis at 90° to the polarizer axis: transmitted intensity?",
      "options": [
        "Full intensity",
        "Half intensity",
        "Zero intensity",
        "Double intensity"
      ],
      "answer": 2,
      "why": "Malus' law gives \\(I = I_1\\cos^2 90^\\circ = 0\\): the axes are crossed."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "label": "Quiz · OMR",
      "time": 60,
      "q": "Plane-polarized light, analyzer turned one full turn: zero occurs how often?",
      "options": [
        "Once",
        "Twice",
        "Four times",
        "Never"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "why": "Zero occurs at 90° and 270°, so twice in one full rotation."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill",
